ection of wall very near the top. Unlike some of the perched villages in this part of Provence, most of the houses in Sainte-Agnes have been restored.One particular highlight in Sainte-Agnes is the Church of Notre-Dame-des-Neiges, dating in part from 1535 although much of the present church is a reconstruction from 1744, hence the baroque design.Another highlight of a visit to Sainte-Agnes is the ruins of a castle built in the 12th century as a retreat from the Saracen invaders. Fort. Craftsmen stores, artists workshops, restaurants and cafés are lined on the old streets in well-restored houses from the 15th and 16th centuries.Notre-Dame des Neiges interior Sainte-Agnes by Tangopaso (Public Domain)Sainte-Agnes rue des Sarrasins by Tangopaso (Public Domain)Sainte-Agnes rue des Sarrasins by Tangopaso (Public Domain)Set above the village atop a jagged peak are the ruins of the Medieval garden of Sainte-Agnes by Tangopaso (Public Domain)Ouvrage de Sainte-Agnes by Tangopaso (Public Domain)A fort built deep into the rock in the 1930s was the southernmost point of the In 1940 it only served fifteen days but had enough firepower to defend itself against Italian fighters.Ouvrage of Sainte-Agnes inside by Tangopaso (Public Domain)Saint-Agnès from jardins Biovès in Menton by Tangopaso (Public Domain)Pierre is a French/Australian who is passionate about France and its culture. As this is not the first time you are here, you may be interested in downloading the guide "20 Amazing Offbeat Places in Paris".People are attracted by Sainte-Agnès for its fantastic position which dominates Great walks in the mountains can be started from Sainte-Agnès, including a two/three hour climb to the Panorama of Menton from Sainte-Agnes © Ondřej Žváček – licence [CC BY-SA 3.0] from Wikimedia CommonsCap-Martin from Sainte-Agnes by Tangopaso (Public Domain)A general view of the perched village of Sainte-Agnès:Sainte-Agnes © Patrick Rouzet – licence [CC BY-SA 3.0] from Wikimedia CommonsLegend has it